[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mNote: the Codex approach gate ran but returned an empty response[0m (the resolved [38;5;153mcodexPath[39m is a stub build, [38;5;153m0.0.0-stub[39m).
  Per the gate's rule, an incomplete call degrades the same as absence — no Codex approaches, noted once, not retried.
  The approaches below are mine alone, with no independent second opinion folded in.

  [1mApproaches[0m

  The real axis of difference is [1mwhere the allowlist is enforced[0m, because that decides what a call site is even able to
  do wrong.

  [1mA. Event catalog — schema per event[0m [3m(recommended)[0m
  A single [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m declaring a catalog: each event name maps to the exact field names permitted for it ([38;5;153mlogin_attempt[39m
  → [38;5;153moutcome[39m, [38;5;153mdurationMs[39m; [38;5;153mvalidation_failed[39m → [38;5;153mreason[39m). Call sites write [38;5;153mlogger.event("login_attempt", { outcome: [39m
  [38;5;153m"success", durationMs: 42 })[39m. The logger looks up the event, keeps only catalog-declared fields, drops the rest
  silently, then attaches level, ISO timestamp, session ID, page URL, and buffers it. Free-text messages aren't part of
  the call signature at all.
  [3mBuys:[0m the deny-by-default guarantee is structural — there is no code path that carries an undeclared value to the
  collector, including one added six months from now. Unknown events are dropped too. It also makes the wire format
  self-describing, which the collector will want.
  [3mCosts:[0m every new log line needs a catalog entry, which is friction by design. Slightly unusual API for anyone
  expecting [38;5;153mlogger.info(...)[39m.

  [1mB. Leveled logger, allowlist at the transport boundary[0m
  Conventional [38;5;153mlogger.info / warn / error(message, fields)[39m, with a single [38;5;153msanitize()[39m applied to [38;5;153mfields[39m just before
  enqueue against one global list of allowed field names.
  [3mBuys:[0m familiar, less ceremony, faster to write, no catalog upkeep.
  [3mCosts:[0m weaker in exactly the way that matters here. The allowlist is global rather than per-event, so any call site
  can attach any allowed field in any context, and the free-text [38;5;153mmessage[39m argument is an unguarded channel —
  [38;5;153mlogger.error(\[39mlogin failed for ${username}`)` sails straight through a field allowlist. Given that avoiding precisely
  that leak is why you chose deny-by-default, this partly undoes the decision you just made.

  [1mC. Console interception[0m — [3mdiscarded, but worth naming so it's not re-proposed:[0m wrapping [38;5;153mconsole.log/error[39m to capture

